  abstract = {Traffic congestion is becoming increasingly severe worldwide, and intelligent transportation systems (ITS) play a vital role in alleviating it. Traffic speed prediction is a core function of ITS, but it remains challenging due to the large-scale traffic speed data and the significant uncertainty in spatiotemporal characteristics, as well as the influence of external factors. To address the limitation that many existing models ignore external influences, this paper proposes EFST-Net, a traffic speed prediction model that integrates weather conditions and point-of-interest (POI) data. The spatial features of traffic speed data are extracted using the Simple and Deep Graph Convolutional Network (GCNII) and Bi-level Global Attention (BGA) modules, while temporal and external factor features are extracted by the Time-series Dense Encoder (TiDE). These features are then fused in the feature fusion module for final prediction. Experiments on the Shenzhen dataset demonstrate that EFST-Net effectively leverages external factors, reducing RMSE by 1.08% to 14.76% compared to baseline models.},
