  abstract = {Accurate prediction of traffic speed is critical for traffic system scheduling, management, and optimization. In highway traffic speed prediction, three essential elements should be considered: 1) the complex spatial diffusion of traffic over time; 2) the influence of traffic patterns on prediction accuracy; and 3) the crucial role of bidirectional learning mechanisms in time series forecasting tasks. To address these challenges, a novel self-supervised spatiotemporal bilateral learning network, named 3S-TBLN, is proposed for long-term traffic speed forecasting. 3S-TBLN adopts a bilateral encoderâdecoder architecture, in which both the encoder and the decoder consist of a semantic transformer, multiple spatio-temporal blocks (ST-Blocks), and a bridge transformer (BridgeTrans). Specifically, the ST-Blocks are designed to model multi-step dynamic spatio-temporal correlations in both the encoder and the decoder. Additionally, in the encoder, BridgeTrans is applied to learn forward traffic patterns from the previous weekâs observations, while the decoder learns reverse patterns in a symmetric manner. Furthermore, a bilateral architectureâbased self-supervised learning strategy is employed to reconstruct historical variables as a pretext task and is jointly optimized with the speed prediction task to learn bidirectional temporal context. Experimental results demonstrate that the proposed 3S-TBLN significantly outperforms state-of-the-art baselines on three real-world highway datasets and effectively addresses the challenge of long-term highway traffic speed prediction. The source code is available at https://github.com/zouguojian/Traffic-speed-prediction/tree/main/3S-TBLN},
