  abstract = {Urban traffic flow prediction is a critical component of intelligent transportation systems, enabling efficient traffic management, congestion mitigation, and enhanced urban planning. This paper presents a novel approach utilizing Spatio-Temporal Graph Neural Networks (ST-GNN) to capture both spatial dependencies and temporal dynamics in traffic networks. By modeling traffic sensors as graph nodes and road segments as edges, the proposed model integrates graph convolutional operations for spatial feature extraction and recurrent neural networks, such as LSTM, for temporal sequence handling. An attention mechanism is incorporated to prioritize relevant features, improving robustness against noisy data in complex urban environments. The model is evaluated on the METR-LA dataset, demonstrating superior performance with Mean Absolute Error (MAE) of 2.82, Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE) of 4.80, and Mean Absolute Percentage Error (MAPE) of 3.41%, outperforming baseline methods like LSTM and ARIMA by approximately 20%. Ablation studies confirm the efficacy of key components, including the graph module and temporal attention. This framework not only achieves high prediction accuracy but also scales efficiently for real-time applications. The results highlight the potential of ST-GNN in advancing traffic prediction technologies, contributing to sustainable urban mobility solutions. Future extensions could incorporate multimodal data sources for even more comprehensive forecasting.},
