  abstract = {Accurate cellular traffic prediction is crucial for the rational allocation of network resources. Existing methods generally overlook the accurate extraction and full utilization of features across different periods of cellular traffic. Thus, we propose a relatively long short-term adaptive spatialâtemporal targeted extraction (RLSASTTE) network, which fully extracts long-term trends and short-term dynamics, and then maximizes their utilization. First, to extract long-term spatialâtemporal features, we exploit the strength of the Transformer in capturing long-range temporal dependencies, address its limitations in spatial relationship modeling by redesigning a spatialâtemporal attention mechanism (STAM), and further adopt a predecomposition strategy to emphasize seasonal component mining. Second, to capture short-term spatialâtemporal features, we define a multidilation convolution to explore short-term dependencies and design a novel local strong correlation dynamic attention (LSCDA) mechanism to investigate local spatial influences, which also eliminates the limitation of fixed neighboring nodes. Finally, we innovatively employ an adaptive dual gating mechanism (ADGM) to efficiently integrate diverse features. We conducted experiments on three real-world datasets. Compared to the state-of-the-art methods, RLSASTTE achieves reductions of at least 3.1% and 3.7% in mean absolute error (MAE) and root mean square error (RMSE), respectively. In addition, we explore auxiliary training based on data augmentation, achieving additional performance improvements of 2.0%â4.5%.},
  publisher = {IEEE},
