  abstract = {Accurate traffic flow prediction plays a pivotal role in executing applications related to intelligent transportation systems (ITS), supporting enhanced urban mobility, efficient traffic management, and well-informed infrastructure planning. This study presents a comparative analysis of two state-of-the-art deep learning models, namely Long Short Term Memory plus (LSTM+) and Attention-based Spatio-Temporal Graph Convolutional Network (ASTGCN), on the publicly available Performance Management System(PEMS) traffic dataset. Model performance is evaluated using standard metrics, including MAE, MRE and RMSE, across multiple prediction time horizons. To promote transparency and model accountability, several Explainable AI (XAI) techniques are executed to interpret model outputs. These interpretability analyses reveal the temporal and spatial features that most significantly influence predictions, highlighting subtle distinctions in model behaviour.Empirical findings demonstrate that the ASTGCN model consistently outperforms LSTM+ across multiple datasets. For instance, on the PEMS03 dataset, ASTGCN achieves a MAE of 19.83 and RMSE of 28.41, outperforming LSTM+ (MAE: 24.61, RMSE: 34.05). A similar trend is also observed in PEMS08, ASTGCN lowers the MAE from 38.19 to 17.20 and MAPE from 22.41% to just 7.88%, demonstrating its strong predictive accuracy.},
