  abstract = {Currently, traffic congestion is becoming increasingly prominent in big cities, and traditional transportation systems struggle to adapt to the traffic surge and the complex allocation of resources. The deep learning technology offers a promising approach to urban traffic congestion prediction. To this end, this paper conducts a comparison study of the performance of different combinations of typical deep learning-based prediction models: LSTM-GRU, GCN-Attention, and GCN-GRU. Based on the experimental results using real trace data of the taxicabs in Beijing, we conclude that the GCN-GRU joint model performs the best, whose average mean absolute error (MAE) between predicted and true values is 1.22, and average structural similarity (SSIM) is 0.9914. This joint model effectively integrates spatiotemporal features to achieve reliable and accurate traffic congestion prediction, offering an excellent reference for the planning and construction of smart transportation systems in big cities.},
