  abstract = {Accurate prediction of railway icing is of great significance for ensuring winter transport safety and optimizing emergency response mechanisms. Under variable weather conditions, the risk of icing is influenced by a combination of local meteorological perturbations and multiscale atmospheric factors. Traditional models have certain limitations in spatial dependence, and this paper proposes a Hierarchical Spatio-Temporal Graph Convolutional Network (HST-GCN) for predicting the risk of highland railway icing. The proposed model first employs K-Means clustering to hierarchically partition the spatial domain and constructs spatial relationships through local- and macro-level graph convolutions with an attention pooling mechanism to identify key regions. In the temporal dimension, gated recurrent units (GRUs) are used to capture the evolutionary characteristics of meteorological variables. Experiments show that the HST-GCN proposed in this paper exhibits better performance in capturing the spatial aggregation and temporal evolution of icing risk than existing mainstream models. The method provides a new way and technical support for intelligent warning of regional meteorological hazards and has the potential to be a valuable tool.},
