  abstract = {This article proposes a deep learning (DL) model based on multi-source heterogeneous spatiotemporal data fusion for traffic flow prediction in the context of satellite communication, MHF-STNet (Multi-source Heterogeneous Fusion Spatio-Temporal Network). Traffic flow prediction has significant spatiotemporal dependence and multi-source heterogeneity characteristics. Future flow is not only influenced by historical flow, but also closely related to various external factors such as spatial correlations between urban areas, weather conditions, and points of interest (POI). To fully explore the deep correlations between these heterogeneous data, this paper first uses clustering methods to identify typical traffic patterns in different regions of the city, and then constructs a unified representation that integrates multimodal data such as traffic flow, geographic location, meteorological information, and POI. By designing a deep neural network (DNN) architecture that integrates attention mechanisms with graph convolutional networks (GCN), the model can effectively capture complex spatiotemporal dynamic features and nonlinear dependencies. The results show that MHF-STNet significantly outperforms traditional prediction methods on multiple real datasets, effectively improving the precision of short-term traffic flow prediction and reducing prediction errors.},
