  abstract = {Multi-mode rail transit networks are composed of stations of various modes and the interconnections between them. Studying passenger flow of a single mode alone makes it difficult to reflect the impact of âfour-network integrationâ and the interactions of cross-mode passenger flows. To fully utilize the spatial structure of passenger flow networks, this paper models the multi-mode rail transit network as a graph structure. By means of Pearson correlation coefficients, line topology, and train frequency between stations from multiple perspectives, the spatial correlation between stations is quantified to construct a station spatial association adjacency matrix. A multi-scale receptive field graph convolutional network (GCN) is designed to extract spatial features, combined with an LSTM network to capture local temporal dependencies and a Transformer network to model global spatiotemporal relationships, forming a GCN+LSTM+Transformer fusion prediction model, achieving high-precision and robust short-term passenger flow prediction for hub stations.},
