  abstract = {Introduction: This paper introduces GraviScan, a novel spatiotemporal framework for predicting defect propagation in semiconductor wafer fabrication. Following the principle of gravitational lensing in astrophysics, the model integrates physics-based simulations with machine learning to achieve accurate prediction of defect propagation compared to conventional approaches. Techniques: We model wafer deformation fields as a dynamic graph, wherein strain deformation is analogous to the bending of light in curved spacetime. GraviScan employs Recurrent Graph Neural Networks (RGNNs) to capture the temporal dynamics of microdefects driven by stress distributions on the wafer surface. Findings: GraviScan accurately predicts potential future defect locations, achieving 92.7% accuracy on both synthetic and real wafer production line datasets. The model achieves a mean lead-time of 3.8 process steps ahead of conventional defect detection methods, enabling proactive intervention. Discussion: The proposed approach yields an 18.5% improvement in wafer yield at the industrial scale, with significant economic benefits and process optimization in semiconductor production. The integration of gravitational lensing analogies with machine learning represents a novel interdisciplinary strategy for advancing defect prediction in manufacturing.},
