  abstract = {Growing interest in applying deep learning techniques to graphs in recent years has resulted in significant advancements in the discipline. Despite the significant success that conventional deep learning approaches have achieved in extracting the properties of Euclidean spatial data. Traditional deep learning techniques still struggle to perform effectively when processing non-Euclidean spatial data. Motivated by the success of several factors, including convolutional networks, recurrent networks, and autoencoders, researchers constructed and utilized a neural network structure to deal with graph data. Graph neural networks (GNNs) are a rapidly growing area of research. Graph neural networks (GNNs) are neural models that capture the dependencies of graphs through message passing between their nodes. In recent years, variants of GNNs, such as graph convolutional networks (GCN), graph attention networks (GAT), and graph recurrent networks (GRN), have demonstrated groundbreaking performances on many deep learning tasks. To further encourage innovations in additional GNN-related applications, it is vital to summarize the most recent studies in this area. This study provides an overview of recent GNN techniques, along with a thorough review of several techniques employed in graph deep learning. Additionally, the existing techniques were grouped into different groups: spatial-temporal GNNs, graph autoencoders, graph convolutional networks, and graph recurrent neural networks. Furthermore, this article also addressed the GNN applications in several fields.},
