  abstract = {Against the backdrop of the global energy transition towards decarbonization, the efficient operation and precise scheduling of natural gas pipeline networks are of paramount importance for energy management in modern smart cities. To address the prevailing challenges of strong heterogeneity, complex spatio-temporal coupling effects, and ultra-long sequential dependencies inherent in multi-station hourly flow data within urban pipeline networks, this paper proposes a novel global deep learning forecasting framework, named ST-MetaNet. This framework innovatively introduces a Station Meta-Embedding mechanism, which learns the static physical and geographical attributes of individual nodes, thereby effectively overcoming the "negative transfer" problem encountered by traditional global models when processing heterogeneous stations. Furthermore, a joint architecture integrating a Dynamic Graph Convolutional Network (DGCN) and a multi-scale ProbSparse Attention mechanism is designed. This architecture not only achieves the adaptive capture of implicit spatial spillover effects within the network topology but also significantly reduces the computational complexity associated with ultra-long historical sequences. This study provides a solid theoretical foundation and an engineering implementation scheme for the intelligent operation, maintenance, and load early-warning of natural gas pipeline networks.},
