  abstract = {Real-time resource forecasting and optimization, including traffic, water, electricity, and other resources, are essential for the intelligent systems required for smart city environments. The non-linear spatial-temporal correlations essential to increase the effectiveness of resource allocation and prompt responses to a variety of events are not well captured by standard models (such as ARIMA, CNN-LSTM, and static GNNs). The proposed system effectively captures changing cross-domain correlations and spatial dependencies by combining temporal attention, multi-source fusion, and dynamic graph neural networks. To achieve a more reliable prediction accuracy and faster operation, it learns attention-based dependencies and dynamically modifies the graph structures. It reduced the RMSE from 7.81 to 6.05 and the MAE from 5.12 to 3.98 in trials. It also reduces energy waste by 18.4% and improves reaction latency by 15%. The results confirm that the framework is flexible, scalable, and reliable enough to support datadriven, forward-thinking resource management and sustainability in smart cities.},
