  abstract = {The development of the Arctic Zone of the Russian Federation (AZRF) requires advanced methods for forecasting and managing workforce flows under conditions of high technological complexity and spatial constraints. Traditional manpower planning tools fail to capture dynamic competency demand, interregional labor mobility, and infrastructure coupling. This paper proposes an integrated neural architecture combining spatio-temporal Graph Neural Networks (ST-GNN), Transformer-based demand forecasting, and an agent-based Reinforcement Learning (RL) behavioral module to support data-driven workforce governance for Arctic industrial projects. The workforce ecosystem is formalized as a heterogeneous multigraph linking labor markets, education systems, infrastructure hubs, and regulatory constraints. As a simulation-based proof of concept, experiments on a synthetic-industrial dataset calibrated to Northwestern Russia indicate an $\mathbf\{1 8\}-\mathbf\{3 3\} \%$ reduction in MAE for competency demand forecasting versus ARIMA and LSTM baselines, a 29.8% RMSE improvement in mobility prediction over gravity model baselines, and system-level effects including a 23% reduction in projected workforce deficits and a $\mathbf\{2 6 \%\}$ decrease in education-industry mismatch. The results support the formation of a reproducible digital twin for Arctic workforce management and align with strategic priorities of industrial operators, educational institutions, and policy stakeholders.},
