  doi = {10.1109/CVCI66304.2025.11348135},
  url = {https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/stamp/stamp.jsp?arnumber=11348135},
  abstract = {In complex traffic scenarios, accurate vehicle trajectory prediction remains challenging due to difficulties in spatiotemporal interaction modeling and uncertainties in driving intentions. To address these limitations, this paper proposes a novel trajectory prediction model based on spatiotemporal feature fusion. The framework employs an LSTM encoder to extract hierarchical trajectory features, where multi-head attention mechanisms and large-kernel convolutions explicitly enhance spatial relationships between vehicles, while a dedicated convolutional modulation module is introduced to optimize temporal feature representation. By incorporating maneuver intention recognition, an LSTM decoder generates multimodal probabilistic predictions over a $\mathbf\{5\}$-second horizon. Evaluations on the NGSIM dataset demonstrate that our trajectory prediction method achieves an average root mean square error (RMSE) of 1.706 meters and an average negative log-likelihood (NLL) of 3.158 within the 5 -second horizon, indicating high prediction accuracy. Meanwhile, the model requires only 13.53 milliseconds (CPU) and 0.023 milliseconds (GPU) inference time on automotive-grade hardware, making it suitable for real-time autonomous driving applications.},
