  abstract = {The accelerating adoption of electric vehicles (EVs) has posed new challenges for power system operation and charging infrastructure planning, particularly in the context of fluctuating urban demand. This paper develops a feature fusion spatio-temporal network (FF-STN) to achieve fine-grained forecasting of EV charging loads at the city scale. The proposed framework incorporates spatially invariant features (e.g., road density, POI distribution, and charging capacity) together with temporally evolving indicators (e.g., demand, travel time, and price) into a unified learning architecture. A residual convolutional module extracts high-level spatial patterns, while a causal temporal convolutional branch captures sequential dependencies. To further mitigate multi-step prediction errors, a day-lag rolling strategy is employed for continuous forecasting. Using real-world data from a public charging station in Shenzhen, China, the proposed method demonstrates superior accuracy and stability over representative baselines, including ARIMA, SVR, LSTM, CNN-LSTM, and GCN. Quantitative results show that FFSTN reduces the mean absolute error by more than 25% compared with the best-performing benchmark, confirming the benefit of combining static and dynamic information for reliable EV load prediction.},
