  abstract = {Traditional urban drainage network flood prediction cannot capture spatiotemporal dynamics due to complex physical topology and heterogeneous multi-source monitoring data. This study proposes a dynamic spatiotemporal graph neural network (ST-GNN) framework. First, the drainage system is abstracted into a directed graph, with manholes as nodes and pipes as edges, to construct its spatial topology. Next, a gated fusion unit based on an attention mechanism is designed to dynamically balance the impact of rainfall input and the internal state of the pipe network, and adaptively learn the spatial dependencies of remote nodes. Finally, by coupling graph convolution with time series convolution, the model simultaneously captures the spatiotemporal dynamics of flood risk propagation. Experiments show that the model achieves an RMSE of 0.071 meters for water level prediction and an F1 score of 0.923 for waterlogging warning, significantly outperforming traditional methods. This demonstrates that the proposed framework can effectively integrate physical structure and dynamic data to achieve more accurate and robust intelligent prediction of urban flood risk.},
