  abstract = {This study addresses challenges of dynamics, uncertainties, and connection reliability bottlenecks in multimodal intermodal transport route planning within modern transportation networks. An integrated framework for dynamic air-rail intermodal routes is proposed, with three key methodologies: (1) XGBoost-based predictive analytics; (2) Spatio-temporal dependency modeling; (3) Multi-Objective Trade-off Mechanism. Based on historical data and network topology, the framework provides a systematic solution with both theoretical value and practical applicability for path planning in dynamic and uncertain multimodal systems such as air-rail networks through the following steps: â The XGBoost model predicts dynamic travel time parameters of critical path segments, reducing RMSE by 43.6% for flight delay and 39.3% for train delay; â¡ A spatio-temporal graph attention network was constructed to incorporate dynamic information such as meteorological data, enabling dynamic refinement of initial predictions from XGBoost. The back-injection parameters led to an 8.64% improvement in the optimal performance metric; â£Based on the optimized prediction parameters, an optimal dynamic route is generated through multi-objective trade-offs tailored to passengers with different preference sensitivities. The framework includes an updating mechanism, improving overall path reliability by 0.06 and achieving a delay reliability metric of 0.97, and demonstrates superior performance in dynamic adaptability and disruption resistance compared to baseline methods. Notably, under scenarios such as adverse weather and peak hours, the transfer reliability metric is improved by 21.3% compared to single-model methods.},
