  abstract = {This study centers on a deep feature synergistic reasoning approach for cross-modal geospatial data, and proposes an innovative solution based on the DFCR framework. The framework realizes deep inference of complex geographic scenes by combining remote sensing images, textual semantics and sensor timing signals through a multidimensional feature synergy mechanism. The study first analyzes the current technical routes for cross-modal geographic data processing, including feature space mapping, graph structure analysis and deep learning-driven approaches, revealing their limitations in spatio-temporal data modeling. To address these shortcomings, the DFCR framework designs a three-branch parallel coding structure and significantly improves the interaction capability and prediction accuracy of multimodal features through an adaptive weight allocation strategy and a dynamic propagation mechanism of spatio-temporal graph inference network. The experimental results show that DFCR outperforms the existing benchmark models on GeoMulti and UrbanSense datasets, and the Rank-1 recognition accuracy and traffic flow prediction error achieve significant reduction, which verifies the effectiveness and robustness of the method in complex geographic scenarios.},
