  abstract = {The rapid advancement of artificial intelligence has accelerated progress in the intelligent vehicle industry. Technologies in this domain demonstrate significant potential for enhancing traffic flow efficiency and driving safety. Downstream decision-making and planning modules can generate more accurate and efficient behavioral strategies via an effective prediction system. This work proposes a spatiotemporal graph network that synergistically integrates graph neural networks (GNN) and LSTM modules. The GNN explicitly encodes spatial interactions among multiple dynamic agents, while the LSTM layers capture temporal dependencies, enabling robust multi-target trajectory prediction. Real-world road data were collected from actual road sweeper operational scenarios and compiled into a dedicated dataset for model training. Validation was performed through dataset evaluation and real-vehicle test validation. Experimental results demonstrate that the GNN-LSTM method reduces the average position error by 0.73 meters at the 5 s time horizon compared with conventional LSTM baselines, with the final position error reduced by $\mathbf\{1. 9 7\}$ meters. The method demonstrates enhanced adaptability for long-term prediction of dynamic targets in low-speed environments.},
